WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.200
Does deplatforming violate a speaker's
First Amendment rights?

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.160
Senator Josh Hawley has repeatedly
claimed that the cancellation of his

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.559
book deal after he challenged the election results

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:17.840
was quote "a direct assault on the First Amendment."

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.199
And others suggest that Twitter violated
President Donald Trump's free speech

00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:26.346
rights by deleting his account. But this is
a common misconception of our rights.

00:00:26.346 --> 00:00:31.679
The First Amendment says that Congress shall
make no law abridging freedom of speech.

00:00:31.679 --> 00:00:36.209
The 14th Amendment extends that
prohibition to state and local government.

00:00:36.209 --> 00:00:41.289
But with rare exceptions, the Constitution does not place
similar restrictions on private actors.

00:00:41.289 --> 00:00:46.049
I can write a letter to the Editor, but
the New York Times is not required to print it.

00:00:46.049 --> 00:00:50.846
In fact, forcing the Times or any other private company
to publish statements against its will

00:00:50.846 --> 00:00:54.480
violates the publisher's own First
Amendment right to decide what it wishes

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.996
to say on its platform. And the same is true of Twitter.

00:00:58.996 --> 00:01:02.879
The First Amendment does not protect speech.
It merely prevents the government from

00:01:02.879 --> 00:01:06.963
deciding who gets to speak and who does not.

00:01:06.963 --> 00:01:11.920
Do you think Twitter's decision to block Trump was just?
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.200
what other topics you'd like our
scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

